# Wick — Debug and automation surface

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Wick carries a small debugging and automation surface, `window.__wick`, so the
game can be driven and inspected from code without holding a key or waiting on
real time.

You implement it. Every operation this file specifies is a deliverable, and the
build installs the finished surface on `window.__wick` as soon as the game has
initialized. Every scenario driven from code reaches the game through it, so it
is present and exactly as specified here.

Each operation is a read or a pose of the game's state. A pose arranges the
night, and the game's own systems run from there exactly as they do in play: an
enemy spawned through the surface is scaled and driven as one the director
spawned, an offer granted through it passes the same candidate rules the
overlay applies, and experience added through it levels the lamplighter exactly
as a gem does.

The surface is inert during normal play, doing nothing until something calls
it.
{{else}}
Wick carries a small debugging and automation surface so the game can be driven
and inspected from code without holding a key or waiting on real time.

You implement it. Every operation this file specifies is a deliverable, and the
build's `initialize` returns the finished surface beside the state it built, as
the pair `[state, debug]`. The engine returns that same value from
`engine.debug`, and it is reached that way alone: nothing is installed on the
page. Every scenario driven from code reaches the game through it, so it is
present and exactly as specified here.

Each operation is a reading or a pose of `WickState`, written in the shape of
`update`, because the engine holds the state by value and nothing holds a
writable one. A pose takes the current state, as `DeepReadonly<WickState>`, and
returns the next `WickState`; a reading takes the state the same way and
returns what it read. A caller drives a pose through the engine's `apply`, as
`engine.apply((s) => debug.start(s))`, and a reading against the engine's
current state, as `debug.snapshot(engine.state)`. A pose arranges the night,
and the game's own systems run from there exactly as they do in play: an enemy
spawned through the surface is scaled and driven as one the director spawned,
and an offer granted through it passes the same candidate rules the overlay
applies. Where the surface's implementation lives under `src/` is yours to
decide; `src/game.ts` declares and exports its type, `WickDebugApi`.

The surface is inert during normal play, doing nothing until something calls
it.
{{/if}}

## A deterministic core

The surface rests on the simulation being deterministic. A run advances on a
fixed tick of `TICK_HZ` (`60`) ticks per second, each worth `TICK_DT` (`1/60`)
seconds: every frame's delta time accumulates, whole ticks are consumed, and
the remainder waits for the next frame, so an interval of game time reaches the
same state however it was divided into frames. Only the `playing` screen ticks.
Every rate in this specification is per second and every duration is in
seconds, and each tick applies exactly `TICK_DT` of them. Two more properties
make the game driveable from code:

- Render-free core. Game state advances from ticks and input alone,
  independent of the canvas, of the frame loop that measured the delta time,
  and of wall-clock time. The dependency runs one way: the simulation reads
  nothing from the renderer, and every hit, kill, drop, and level-up is decided
  from the state alone.
- Seeded randomness. The game holds one pseudo-random generator, seeded by
  `reset` and keeping its whole state in `rngState`, and every random draw
  comes from it: a spawn's angle and type, an offer draw, a puddle's landing
  point, a strike's target, a swarm's direction, a chest's fallback item, and
  the bread and draft draws. The order in which the systems draw from it is
  yours.

Given the same seed, the same sequence of calls, and the same number of ticks,
the game reaches the same `run` and `rngState` every time. `simTime` and
`muted` stand outside that: `simTime` accumulates the delta time of every
update whatever the screen, and `muted` mirrors the runtime.

Audio belongs to the ticks and the frames. A pose changes the state alone and
sounds nothing; the cues a scenario hears come from the ticks run afterwards.

## What the runtime provides instead

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The keyboard and the overlay belong to the runtime layer beneath the game, and
the surface carries no operation for the menu actions or for the overlay. That
layer delivers the actions the game registers from the key bindings this
specification fixes, and it draws and toggles the debug overlay.

The clock is the exception, because nothing outside this build owns it. The
surface is what takes the game off real time and advances it, through
`setAutoStep` and `step` below. Movement is the other exception: `setInput`
stands in for the held movement keys, so a scenario walks the lamplighter
without a key event.
{{else}}
The clock, the keyboard, and the overlay belong to the {{engine.name}} engine,
which documents them in the `engine/` directory at the root of this repository,
and the surface carries no operation for any of them. The engine advances the
game frame by frame and can take it off real time, so a scenario pairs a clock
that supplies its own deltas with `engine.advance`, and a `ConstantClock` of
`1000 / TICK_HZ` milliseconds steps exactly one tick per frame. It drives the
actions the game registers, so a scenario walks the lamplighter by dispatching
key events at the engine's event target, or places it with `setPlayer` below.
It draws and toggles the debug overlay.

The two looping cues are reconciled by `update` on every frame from the state
it is handed, through the engine's `looping`, `loop`, and `stop`, so a run
started through `start` sounds exactly as one started from the menu.
{{/if}}

What the overlay shows is Wick's, under Diagnostics below.

## The operations

The surface carries `version` (`WICK_DEBUG_VERSION`, `1`), a plain number, and
the operations below. Values are plain numbers, strings, booleans, and plain
objects. `x` and `y` are world units, on the plane whose origin is where the
run began, and every duration is in seconds.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
{{else}}

Each operation's first parameter is the current state,
`DeepReadonly<WickState>`. A pose returns the next `WickState`, built from the
one it was handed, which it leaves as it was; a reading returns what it read.
The headings below name the parameters that follow the state.
{{/if}}

No operation decides an outcome. Hits, kills, gems, experience, level-ups, and
evolutions come from stepping the real systems after a pose, and an operation
whose precondition does not hold leaves the state exactly as it was, so a
scenario reads the outcome of any pose from `snapshot`.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `setAutoStep(enabled)`

Takes the game off real time, and gives it back. `setAutoStep(false)` stops the
frame loop feeding the wall clock's delta time into the tick accumulator, so
the run advances only when `step` says so. `setAutoStep(true)` returns it to
running itself, which is how a build starts and how it is played.

Drawing is unaffected either way: the loop keeps rendering, so the canvas shows
the state the most recent tick left.

### `step(ticks)`

Runs `ticks` whole simulation ticks, immediately and in order, each the full
tick followed by a render. `ticks` defaults to `1`. It runs on `playing`, and a
tick that opens an overlay or ends the run is the last one the call runs; the
ticks not run are not carried over. On any other screen the call changes
nothing, since nothing ticks there.

Stepping while the game is still stepping automatically adds to what the wall
clock is already doing, so a scenario that must be reproducible calls
`setAutoStep(false)` first.

{{/if}}
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `reset(options)`
{{else}}
### `reset(state, options)`
{{/if}}

Restores every declared field of the game's state to its title-screen value:
the title screen with its first item highlighted, the idle run `specs/state.md`
gives, the tick accumulator and `simTime` at `0`. `options.seed` seeds
`rngState`, defaulting to `DEFAULT_SEED` (`1`). `muted` stays as it is.
{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
Any looping cue stops.
{{else}}
The next `update` stops any looping cue, since the state it restores holds no
run.
{{/if}}

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `snapshot()`

A pure read of the state, returned as the plain object under Snapshot shape
below. It changes nothing.

### `start()`
{{else}}
### `snapshot(state)`

A pure reading of `state`, returned as the plain object under Snapshot shape
below. It returns no state, because it poses nothing.

### `start(state)`
{{/if}}

Poses exactly what `LIGHT THE LAMP` does, from any screen: a fresh run, which
is the idle run of `specs/state.md` with Taper at level `1` and cooldown `0` in
the first weapon slot, `screen` at `playing`, `menuIndex` at `0`, and
`autoSpawn` `true`. `rngState` and `simTime` stay as they are, so a run from a
known seed is a `reset` followed by this.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `setAutoSpawn(enabled)`
{{else}}
### `setAutoSpawn(state, enabled)`
{{/if}}

Turns the spawn director on or off: the window spawns and the scripted events
together. Turning it off removes nothing already alive, and turning it on
resumes the director where its timer and its fired events stand. It applies on
`playing` and `paused`.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `spawnEnemy(type, x, y)`
{{else}}
### `spawnEnemy(state, type, x, y)`
{{/if}}

Spawns one enemy of `type`, an `EnemyId`, centered at world `(x, y)` through
the real spawn path: its `maxHp` is scaled by the run clock exactly as the
director's spawns are, it takes the next id, and a drifting enemy's heading is
the unit vector from `(x, y)` toward the lamplighter's center. It applies on
`playing`, and the enemy appears in the next snapshot.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `clearEnemies()`
{{else}}
### `clearEnemies(state)`
{{/if}}

Removes every enemy alive, elites and the Dark included. Nothing drops, nothing
counts as a kill, and every other field stays as it was. It applies on
`playing`.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `spawnPickup(kind, x, y)`
{{else}}
### `spawnPickup(state, kind, x, y)`
{{/if}}

Places one item at world `(x, y)` with the next id. `kind` is a `GemTier`, which
places a gem of that tier, unattracted, or a `PickupKind`, which places that
pickup. It applies on `playing`.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `grant(id)`
{{else}}
### `grant(state, id)`
{{/if}}

Applies exactly what accepting the offer `id` would apply, through the accept
path, outside any overlay: a weapon or passive not held joins the first free
slot of its kind at level `1`, a held one gains one level with every effect a
level brings, and `LAMP_OIL_ID` (`"lamp-oil"`) heals `LAMP_OIL_HEAL` (`30`),
capped at `maxHp`, whatever the candidate pool holds. An `id` that is not a
legal candidate at the moment of the call leaves the state as it was: the slot
kind is full, the item is at its maximum level, the id is an evolved weapon, or
the id is a base weapon whose evolution is held. It applies on `playing`.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `setOffers(ids)`
{{else}}
### `setOffers(state, ids)`
{{/if}}

Sets what the next level-up overlay presents, in the order given, in place of
the random draw. The list is checked against the candidate pool at the moment
that overlay opens, and it is accepted when it holds `1` to `OFFER_COUNT`
(`3`) distinct ids, each a candidate of the pool at that moment, or holds
`LAMP_OIL_ID` alone while the pool is empty; the overlay then presents exactly
that list. Any other list is discarded whole and the overlay draws at random.
One overlay consumes it, and a later overlay draws at random again. It applies
on `playing`.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `addXp(amount)`
{{else}}
### `addXp(state, amount)`
{{/if}}

Adds `amount` experience, as it stands and with no multiplier, through the
real gain path: every level-up the total earns queues, overflow carries, and
the level-up overlay opens on the spot exactly as it does for a gem. It applies
on `playing`.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `choose(index)`
{{else}}
### `choose(state, index)`
{{/if}}

On `levelup`, accepts the offer at `index`, counted from `0` down the list,
exactly as moving the highlight there and pressing `confirm` would: the item is
applied, and either the next queued overlay opens with a fresh pool or play
resumes. An `index` outside the offers leaves the state as it was.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `setPlayer(patch)`
{{else}}
### `setPlayer(state, patch)`
{{/if}}

Applies `patch`, an object of any of `x`, `y`, `hp`, and `facing`, directly to
the lamplighter as a precondition, leaving every field the patch omits as it
was. `hp` is capped at `maxHp`, and `facing` is `"left"` or `"right"`. It
applies on `playing`.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `setTime(seconds)`
{{else}}
### `setTime(state, seconds)`
{{/if}}

Sets the run clock: `tick` becomes `round(seconds × TICK_HZ)`. Everything the
clock derives follows from the next tick on: the spawn window, the health
scaling of later spawns, and the scripted events, so every event whose time is
at or before the new clock is marked fired without spawning anything, and an
event whose time is after it fires when the clock reaches it. When the window
changes, `spawnTimer` becomes `0` as on any window change. It applies on
`playing`.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `setInput(held)`

Holds movement actions from code. `held` is an object of any of `up`, `down`,
`left`, and `right`, each a boolean; an action the object names is held or
released as given, an action it omits is released, and the values persist until
the next call. While any action is held this way the movement the game reads on
`playing` comes from here in place of the keyboard, so the lamplighter walks
exactly as it does under held keys, the facing included. `setInput({})`
releases every action and hands movement back to the keyboard.

### `pause()`, `resume()`
{{else}}
### `pause(state)`, `resume(state)`
{{/if}}

`pause` poses the pause action on `playing`, moving to `paused`. `resume`
poses it on `paused`, returning to `playing`. On any other screen each leaves
the state as it was.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `openChest()`
{{else}}
### `openChest(state)`
{{/if}}

On `playing`, poses collecting a chest: the chest overlay opens with the same
result collecting one produces, an evolution of the first eligible weapon in
slot order, a level for a random held item below its maximum, or the heal, and
`chestResult` reports which.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
### `closeOverlay()`
{{else}}
### `closeOverlay(state)`
{{/if}}

Poses `confirm` on an overlay. On `levelup` it accepts the highlighted offer,
exactly as `choose(menuIndex)` does; on `chest` it closes the overlay and play
resumes. On any other screen it leaves the state as it was.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
A typical scenario calls `reset` with a seed and `start`, `setAutoStep(false)`,
`setAutoSpawn(false)` to keep the director out of the way, `spawnEnemy` and
`grant` to pose the encounter wanted, `setInput` to walk, then `step` through
it, reading positions, health, and kills from `snapshot()` as it goes.
{{else}}
A typical scenario applies `reset` with a seed and `start` through
`engine.apply`, applies `setAutoSpawn(false)` to keep the director out of the
way, `spawnEnemy` and `grant` to pose the encounter wanted, then steps the
engine's own clock through it, reading positions, health, and kills from
`debug.snapshot(engine.state)` as it goes.

## `WickDebugApi`

`src/game.ts` declares and exports the surface's type exactly as it stands
here, so a caller written against it drives any build the same way.
`WickSnapshot` is the type of the object under Snapshot shape below, declared
by the build to match that shape field for field.

```ts
export interface WickDebugApi {
  readonly version: number;
  reset(
    state: DeepReadonly<WickState>,
    options?: { readonly seed?: number },
  ): WickState;
  snapshot(state: DeepReadonly<WickState>): WickSnapshot;
  start(state: DeepReadonly<WickState>): WickState;
  setAutoSpawn(state: DeepReadonly<WickState>, enabled: boolean): WickState;
  spawnEnemy(
    state: DeepReadonly<WickState>,
    type: EnemyId,
    x: number,
    y: number,
  ): WickState;
  clearEnemies(state: DeepReadonly<WickState>): WickState;
  spawnPickup(
    state: DeepReadonly<WickState>,
    kind: GemTier | PickupKind,
    x: number,
    y: number,
  ): WickState;
  grant(state: DeepReadonly<WickState>, id: OfferId): WickState;
  setOffers(
    state: DeepReadonly<WickState>,
    ids: readonly OfferId[],
  ): WickState;
  addXp(state: DeepReadonly<WickState>, amount: number): WickState;
  choose(state: DeepReadonly<WickState>, index: number): WickState;
  setPlayer(
    state: DeepReadonly<WickState>,
    patch: {
      readonly x?: number;
      readonly y?: number;
      readonly hp?: number;
      readonly facing?: Facing;
    },
  ): WickState;
  setTime(state: DeepReadonly<WickState>, seconds: number): WickState;
  pause(state: DeepReadonly<WickState>): WickState;
  resume(state: DeepReadonly<WickState>): WickState;
  openChest(state: DeepReadonly<WickState>): WickState;
  closeOverlay(state: DeepReadonly<WickState>): WickState;
}
```
{{/if}}

## Snapshot shape

`snapshot` returns:

```js
{
  version: 1,
  screen: "title" | "howto" | "playing" | "levelup"
        | "chest" | "paused" | "fallen" | "dawn",
  menuIndex: <number>,
  run: {
    tick: <number>,
    time: <number>,                // tick / TICK_HZ, seconds
    level: <number>,
    xp: <number>,
    xpToNext: <number>,            // XP_BASE + XP_STEP * (level - 1)
    kills: <number>,
    player: { x, y, facing: "left" | "right", hp },
    maxHp: <number>,
    armor: <number>,
    moveSpeed: <number>,           // units per second
    pickupRadius: <number>,
    weapons: [{ id, level, cooldown }],
    passives: [{ id, level }],
    enemies: [
      { id, type, x, y, hp, maxHp, heading: { x, y }, age, contactCooldown },
    ],
    projectiles: [
      { id, weapon, x, y, vx, vy, ax, ay, radius, ttl, pierce,
        hits: [{ enemy, cooldown }] },
    ],
    zones: [
      { id, weapon, kind, x, y, radius, width?, height?, ttl,
        hits: [{ enemy, cooldown }] },
    ],
    gems: [{ id, tier, x, y, attracted }],
    pickups: [{ id, kind, x, y }],
    offers: [<string>],            // the open level-up overlay's offers
    nextOffers: [<string>] | null, // what setOffers queued
    pendingLevelUps: <number>,
    chestResult: { kind: "evolve", weapon }
      | { kind: "level", item, level }
      | { kind: "heal" }
      | null,
    spawnTimer: <number>,
    spawnWindow: <number>,         // floor(time / SPAWN_WINDOW)
    autoSpawn: <boolean>,
    firedEvents: [<number>],       // run-clock seconds, ascending
    aliveCommons: <number>,
    nextId: <number>,
  },
  muted: <boolean>,
  accumulator: <number>,           // frame time waiting for the next tick
  simTime: <number>,               // accumulated simulation time, in seconds
  rngState: <number>,
}
```

The shape is fixed, and every field is present whatever the screen. `run`
reports the idle run of `specs/state.md` on `title` and `howto`, and the run
that just ended on `fallen` and `dawn`. `zones[].ttl` is `null` for a zone that
never expires, and `width` and `height` appear on a slash alone. Every other
field, `accumulator` included, is read straight off the game's state, so what
the snapshot reports is what the game holds. Eight are derived rather than
stored:

| Field | Derived from |
| --- | --- |
| `time` | `tick / TICK_HZ` |
| `xpToNext` | `XP_BASE` (`5`) `+ XP_STEP` (`10`) `× (level − 1)` |
| `maxHp` | `BASE_MAX_HP` (`100`) `+ TALLOW_HP_PER_LEVEL` (`15`) `×` the Tallow level held |
| `armor` | `BRASS_ARMOR_PER_LEVEL` (`1`) `×` the Brass level held |
| `moveSpeed` | `MOVE_SPEED` (`180`) `× (1 + BELLOWS_SPEED_PER_LEVEL` (`0.1`) `×` the Bellows level held`)` |
| `pickupRadius` | `PICKUP_RADIUS` (`48`) `× (1 + LURE_PICKUP_PER_LEVEL` (`0.25`) `×` the Lure level held`)` |
| `spawnWindow` | `min(19, floor(time / SPAWN_WINDOW))`, with `SPAWN_WINDOW` (`30`) |
| `aliveCommons` | how many of `enemies` have `rank` `common` in `ENEMIES` and are not `gnat`, the count `specs/enemies.md` holds against the cap |

A passive not held counts as level `0` in every formula above. Two fields need
the game to keep them honest every frame:

- `muted` mirrors the runtime's mute bit, refreshed in every update, since the
  runtime owns muting and this is the game's readable copy of it.
- `simTime` accumulates the delta time of every update, whatever the screen.

## Diagnostics

The runtime's debug overlay shows the values the game registers with it as
diagnostic sources. Register at least the current `screen`, the run clock, the
level with `xp` over `xpToNext`, `hp` over `maxHp`, the kill count, the
lamplighter's position and facing, the enemy count with the spawn window
index, the projectile and zone counts, the gem count, the held weapons with
their levels and cooldowns, the held passives with their levels, `autoSpawn`,
and `pendingLevelUps`, the same facts the snapshot reports. Keep each one short
enough to read on a line, and keep every source a pure read, so watching the
overlay leaves the game as it is.

{{#if (eq engine.slug "none")}}
The overlay is part of the runtime layer you write. It draws the registered
sources, it is shown and hidden by the backtick key (`KeyboardEvent.code`
`Backquote`), and it reads the game without changing it.
{{else}}
Registering those values is the whole of Wick's part, through
`InitApi.diagnostics`. Each source is called with the state current at the
read, as `DeepReadonly<WickState>`, and reads off that argument: a source that
closed over the state `initialize` built would report the title screen forever,
because every frame leaves a new value behind. Drawing the panel, toggling it,
and keeping it read-only are the engine's.
{{/if}}
